WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.305
Excessive data exposure occurs when an API
returns more information than the caller

2
00:00:09.385 --> 00:00:11.989
needs or should receive.

3
00:00:12.069 --> 00:00:20.501
This often happens when backend models are
serialized directly into responses.

4
00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:27.898
A database entity may include internal
IDs, role flags, audit fields,

5
00:00:27.978 --> 00:00:32.566
tenant metadata, email addresses, tokens,

6
00:00:32.646 --> 00:00:38.474
or configuration details that the client
never needed.

7
00:00:38.554 --> 00:00:46.491
API responses should be shaped
intentionally for the caller and operation.

8
00:00:46.571 --> 00:00:51.531
Field-level filtering is a practical
control.

9
00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:59.300
The server should decide which fields are
returned based on the endpoint,

10
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.092
caller, tenant, relationship, and purpose.

11
00:01:04.172 --> 00:01:09.877
A user profile response, an administrative
response,

12
00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:18.513
and an internal service response may all
represent the same underlying object but

13
00:01:18.593 --> 00:01:21.321
expose different fields.

14
00:01:21.401 --> 00:01:28.842
Do not rely on the client to hide
sensitive fields after receiving them.

15
00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:35.122
If the caller should not see it, the API
should not return it.

16
00:01:35.202 --> 00:01:41.031
Error message leakage is another form of
data exposure.

17
00:01:41.111 --> 00:01:49.543
Stack traces, database errors, exception
names, service URLs, debug metadata,

18
00:01:49.623 --> 00:01:55.824
and raw downstream responses can reveal
system internals.

19
00:01:55.904 --> 00:02:04.708
APIs should provide stable error
structures that support troubleshooting without

20
00:02:04.788 --> 00:02:08.012
exposing unnecessary detail.

21
00:02:08.092 --> 00:02:16.649
Correlation IDs can help operators find
internal logs while keeping the external

22
00:02:16.729 --> 00:02:18.465
response clean.

23
00:02:18.545 --> 00:02:25.737
Pagination, sorting, and filtering
controls also affect exposure.

24
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:33.878
Unbounded result sets can reveal too much
data and create availability risk.

25
00:02:33.958 --> 00:02:42.019
Flexible filters can accidentally allow
enumeration of sensitive records.

26
00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:46.067
Sort fields can leak internal schema.

27
00:02:46.147 --> 00:02:53.835
A secure API response design defines
default limits, maximum page sizes,

28
00:02:53.915 --> 00:02:58.628
allowed filter fields, allowed sort
fields,

29
00:02:58.708 --> 00:03:04.660
and stable response shapes that match real
client needs.
